                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "First things first; this is not a game you can play a couple hours and form an opinion.  I mean you can, but you'd be mistaken.  You need at least 10 hours of gameplay before you can decide whether it is for you or not.  I don't make the rules ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯\n\nWhen you start your character can barely fight the smallest, weakest monsters on map.  Don't even think about going 1v3 against bandits.  You should just avoid or run away when detected.  Duras is a huge help in the beginning.  If you leave your companion and run away there is no relationship penalty for that.  They just you later.\n\nSo if you cannot fight, what do you do to progress?  You get some experience when you kill enemies, but progress is pretty much tied to completing quests.  Just complete the talking quests in the beginning and try to reach The Dome. Mid-game you get better gear and you are able to fight mostly anyone.  Combat is challenging at this stage.  End-game you are OP, and you get to experience the fantasy of dominance for a while.  There are still some enemies that require finesse though.\n\nTBH combat is all over the place in this game.  Controls are frustrating when you lock on but they are also frustrating when you don't lock on.  Two handed weapons are useless.  I mean useless for the player character, due to controls.  They are very effective when yielded by enemies.  I went for a sword and board setup in the beginning, mostly because those were what I could get my hands on.  Shield sometimes blocks, I think.  Frustrated with meelee I switched to rifles.  Ranged combat is not much better TBH.  Rifles get more viable near end-game though.  One weapon that is available (if you have the money) and effective throught the game are grenades.  Stock up on grenades at all times, they're seriously OP.\n\nAlso one final combat tip: I did not feel bad for cheesing combat encounters, mostly using the height advantage via jetpack.  You should not too.  Note that when there is no meelee path, all enemies have some kind of ranged attack.  You still need to dodge those somehow.\n\nDialogue is fully voiced, voice acting is okay.  But there is way too much dialogue in this title.  The NPC's like to talk a bit too much.  Luckily the quests, including the main quest is interesting.  Like FONV, but maybe not quite at the same level.  To my surprise, ending was good too.  I expected something blander but it was good.  When the main story ends, you are expected to visit a number of NPC's though.  That part was absolutely ridiculous.  It should have been a cutscene or something or cut altogether as a mission.\n\nThere is progression in terms of attributes and skills, but the real progression is the faction armor.  You can only get decent armor from the faction you've joined and you get stronger only when you wear a stronger armor.  Yes, your attributes and skills also improve along with that and you get to deal more DPS, but, ultimately, the way I see it, it is all tied to armor.  It is not bad, you just need to adjust your expectations accordingly.  Join a faction as soon as possible and do the faction quests.  It is not optional.\n\nThere are a lot of review calling this game \"too difficult\".  I do not enjoy difficulty for difficulty sake.  This game is very difficult if you try to ignore the level requirements.  It is a fun and worthwhile RPG, jank and all, I suggest giving it a try.\n",
